# S12.1 final review: backup-to-marker writer exclusion

Date: 2026-09-30
Scope: Control PR #203, final same-SHA review P1

## Finding

The prior controller validated a point-in-time backup before writing the
durable deployment marker, but installed the root-owned Git recovery barriers
only afterwards. A short ordinary `chatops` Git command could therefore begin
after backup validation, complete before the later barrier, and be overwritten
by the canonical release sync without being represented in the rollback
backup.

## Root cause

Backup generation and canonical repository mutation used separate serialized
regions. Process-count checks bounded both regions but could not provide a
continuous exclusion guarantee across their gap.

## Correction

The controller now records only repository path metadata before entering the
barrier. It then holds one uninterrupted root-owned writer exclusion region
across:

1. repository-state and bundle capture;
2. post-capture backup validation;
3. durable attempt-marker creation;
4. exact canonical release sync; and
5. immutable release-stage creation.

Backup and sync Git commands explicitly use the quarantined Git directories.
Dirty-state checks exclude only that fixed, prevalidated quarantine path;
ordinary untracked paths remain blocking and are covered by a negative test.
Freeze verification moves to the immutable staged release after the barrier is
removed. If any pre-marker step fails, the barrier is restored without
starting rollback because no canonical deployment mutation has begun.

## Rollback and safety

The existing exactly-once rollback remains armed only after the durable attempt
marker. The barrier is restored on every context exit, including exceptions.
No provider credential, identity material or runtime server state is changed by
this source correction.
